<h2 id="pallet-heading">Pallet Types &amp; Dispenser Compatibility</h2>
<p>Pallet dispensers must handle the specific pallet type used in your operation. Pallet material, size, and condition affect dispenser design and reliability:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wood GMA pallets (48 x 40 in):</strong> The most common pallet in North America. New wood pallets interlock tightly and require higher separation force. Recycled pallets vary in height and condition, broken boards, protruding nails, and warped decks cause mechanical dispenser jams.</li>
<li><strong>Plastic pallets:</strong> Consistent dimensions and weight. Nest tightly when stacked. Some designs include anti-nesting features (bumps or offsets) that aid mechanical separation. Easier to dispense than wood pallets.</li>
<li><strong>EUR pallets (1200 x 800 mm):</strong> Standard in Europe. <a href="https://www.epal-pallets.org/eu-en/load-carriers/epal-euro-pallet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPAL specifies</a> the Euro pallet at 1200 x 800 x 144 mm. Four-way entry. Dispenser magazine requires adjustment from GMA dimensions.</li>
<li><strong>Custom/industrial pallets:</strong> Oversized, undersized, or non-standard pallets require custom dispenser sizing. Robotic dispensers adapt most easily to custom pallet dimensions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pallet quality is a major reliability variable. Operations using recycled wood pallets should budget for higher dispenser maintenance and consider inline pallet inspection (photoeye or vision-based) to reject damaged pallets before they enter the dispenser. For a full discussion of <a href="/industries/warehouse-conveyors/">warehouse and distribution conveyor systems</a>, see our dedicated guide.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Conveyor Integration</h2>
<p>The pallet dispenser connects to the palletizing system through pallet conveyors, typically <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">chain-driven live roller (CDLR)</a> or chain conveyor rated for the heaviest pallet or pallet stack it will carry. The conveyor carries individual pallets from the dispenser to the palletizer load station. Key integration points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pallet centering:</strong> Guide rails or centering devices on the outfeed conveyor align the pallet before it reaches the palletizer. Misaligned pallets cause stacking errors.</li>
<li><strong>Pallet presence detection:</strong> Photoeye sensors confirm a pallet is in position at the palletizer before the palletizer cycles. The dispenser feeds the next pallet only when the previous pallet has cleared.</li>
<li><strong>Stack height management:</strong> The dispenser PLC tracks the number of pallets remaining in the stack and signals for a refill (forklift alert, tower light, or SCADA notification) when the count reaches a set threshold.</li>
<li><strong>Slip sheet dispensing:</strong> Some palletizer systems require a slip sheet (corrugated or plastic) between the pallet and the first layer of cases. A slip sheet dispenser works alongside the pallet dispenser, placing a sheet on each empty pallet before it reaches the palletizer.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on end-of-line conveyor integration including <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a>, sealing, and palletizing, see our packaging line conveyor guides. For cost considerations, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
